The rear camera's exposure ceiling follows the frame length

THE SYMPTOM
-----------
Lengthening the frame did not give more light. Vertical blanking goes in and
stays set, but the exposure ceiling does not move:

    v4l2-ctl -c vertical_blanking=6000     <- goes in, and reads back 6000
    exposure ... min=6 max=1632            <- does not move

1632 is 1680 of vts_def minus 48 of margin, that is, the MODE's limit. With that,
in a normal room and with the gain already at maximum, the signal stayed at 22
counts out of 255.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS
---------------------------------
With the image down in the sensor's noise, contrast autofocus CANNOT converge,
and not because of the motor: the noise is added AFTER the optics, so it does not
defocus. The hill comes out flat, the reticle comes out red, and everything works
perfectly except that there is nothing to measure.

It took several rounds of measurements before I understood it.

THE CAUSE
---------
`imx682_set_ctrl()` writes the new frame length on receiving V4L2_CID_VBLANK:

    case V4L2_CID_VBLANK:
    	ret = imx682_write16(s, IMX682_REG_FRAME_LENGTH,
    			     s->mode->height + ctrl->val);

...but it does not touch the exposure range, which is only set when changing
mode, using the DEFAULT vts. So the sensor accepts the long frame and the control
still believes the frame is the short one.

It is the same fault the hi1337 had, and which was fixed there in patches 0180
and 0181.

THE FIX
-------
Move the exposure range when the vertical blanking changes, with the ceiling set
to height + blanking - margin, and preserving the current value, clamped.

AND IT GOES BEFORE THE pm_runtime_get_if_in_use, which is the detail that
matters. That call is on the function's first line and RETURNS if the sensor is
powered down. Putting the adjustment after it, the range would not be updated
with the camera stopped -which is exactly when the knobs get adjusted- and any
exposure requested afterwards would be silently clipped against the old limit. It
is the same order imx219 and company use in mainline, and for the same reason.

The minmax.h include is also added, for clamp_t: it was arriving transitively,
but that means relying on a chain of headers that can change.

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/imx682.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/imx682.c
@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/i2c.h>
+#include <linux/minmax.h>	/* clamp_t */
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
@@ -1027,6 +1028,33 @@
 	struct imx682 *s = container_of(ctrl->handler, struct imx682, ctrls);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * THE EXPOSURE CEILING FOLLOWS THE FRAME LENGTH
+	 *
+	 * The exposure cannot exceed the frame, that is height plus vertical
+	 * blanking. The range was only set when changing mode, with the default
+	 * vts, so lengthening the frame did nothing: measured, with
+	 * vertical_blanking=6000 accepted, `exposure` stayed at max=1632
+	 * -1680 of vts_def minus 48 of margin-.
+	 *
+	 * In low light that leaves the image as sensor noise, and the noise is NOT
+	 * blurred because it is added after the optics: autofocus
+	 * cannot converge no matter how well the lens moves.
+	 *
+	 * It goes BEFORE the pm_runtime_get_if_in_use below on purpose. That one
+	 * returns if the sensor is powered off, and the range has to be moved
+	 * ANYWAY: whoever adjusts the controls usually does it with the camera
+	 * stopped, and if the ceiling does not find out, the exposure requested later
+	 * is silently clamped against the old limit.
+	 */
+	if (ctrl->id == V4L2_CID_VBLANK) {
+		int ceiling = s->mode->height + ctrl->val - IMX682_EXPOSURE_MARGIN;
+		int value = clamp_t(int, s->exposure->val, s->mode->exp_min, ceiling);
+
+		__v4l2_ctrl_modify_range(s->exposure, s->mode->exp_min, ceiling,
+					 IMX682_EXPOSURE_STEP, value);
+	}
+
 	/* With no power there is nobody to write to; this is not an error. */
 	if (!pm_runtime_get_if_in_use(&s->client->dev))
 		return 0;
